    <section id="ideas" class="home-section">
      <h2>Learn the ideas before you build</h2>
      <p class="section-lede">Five ideas decide your moves. Read them once. Return when a result needs the reason.</p>

      <h3>1. A second brain is a product with a door</h3>
      <p>Chat scrap disappears. A vault that only one role may write through a named tool becomes something you can audit, baseline, and hand to this afternoon&#x27;s intake fight. MCP is the sanctioned door. The boundary is larger than the door: project topology, per-agent permissions, denied filesystem tools, ask-gated writes, human approval, and tool receipts.</p>

      <h3>2. Parallel dispatch is assumed — retrieval is new</h3>
      <p>You already dispatched workers yesterday. Do not re-learn that skill here. Spend the morning on note schema, hub structure, director merge, cold query, and audit. Keep worker dispatch short.</p>

      <h3>3. Citations must be heavy enough to grade</h3>
      <p>Each note carries source ID, raw path, file sha256, publisher, dates, locator, exact excerpt, claim, confidence, uncertainty, and tags for route leg, mode, factor, and threat class. A note without that package does not enter the brain.</p>

      <h3>4. Threat material ends in protection language</h3>
      <p>Open sources describe interference classes against rail, port, and sealift. Your notes answer what to <strong>protect</strong>, <strong>detect</strong>, and <strong>recover</strong>. They do not rank targets, teach access methods, or optimize attack effects.</p>

      <h3>5. Markdown cannot prove the write path</h3>
      <p>Anyone can drop a file into a folder. Capture MCP receipts and the permission snapshot. The verifier reads those evidence files. P5 will later freeze path hashes of the finished vault; that freeze is integrity, not authorship.</p>
    </section>

